Scenario/Case # 2

By using a Partial keyword,you make all the key optionals in type
type Cup={
    name:string;
    type:string
}

const updates=(item:Partial<Cup>)=>{
    console.log(item);
    
}

updates({name:"kesa"})

One problem in using partial is that you can only send a empty object also
updates({})

Scenario/Case # 3

By using a Required keyword,you make all the key required in type even if you use optional sign (?) in it

type Cup={
    name?:string;
    type?:string
}

const updates=(item:Required<Cup>)=>{
    console.log(item);
    
}

updates({name:"kesa",type:"tea"})

Scenario/Case # 4

By using a Pick keyword,you can pick some selective values from any type.
type Cup={
    name:string;
    type:string;
    price:number
}

type BasicCup=Pick<Cup,"name"|"price">   //now in BasicCup,only name & price are compulsory

let example:BasicCup={
    name:"danedar",
    price:34
}

Scenario/Case # 5

By using a Omit keyword,you can omit/remove some selective values from any type.

type Cup={
    name:string;
    type:string;
    price:number
}

type BasicCup=Omit<Cup,"type">    //now except type key,all other values has been declared for type BasicCup

let example:BasicCup={
    name:"danedar",
    price:34
}

